1
00:00:00,081 --> 00:00:02,602
Fifth of January, 2024.

2
00:00:03,384 --> 00:00:07,566
At twelve minutes and thirty-three
seconds past five in the afternoon, a

3
00:00:07,606 --> 00:00:14,730
Boeing 737-9 climbed through
14,830 feet over Portland, Oregon.

4
00:00:15,425 --> 00:00:17,484
Then the cabin pressure dropped.

5
00:00:17,903 --> 00:00:20,082
The cabin altitude warning sounded.

6
00:00:20,602 --> 00:00:23,141
One second later, the master caution.

7
00:00:23,764 --> 00:00:27,761
The captain would describe it
as an explosive experience.

8
00:00:28,210 --> 00:00:31,436
The first officer said
simply: it was chaos.

9
00:00:32,289 --> 00:00:36,678
Behind the wing, on the left side,
there was now a hole in the fuselage.

10
00:00:37,057 --> 00:00:41,363
A rectangle about 29 inches
wide and 59 inches high.

11
00:00:41,869 --> 00:00:43,987
The two seats beside it were empty.

12
00:00:44,731 --> 00:00:49,221
Fourteen minutes later, Alaska
Airlines flight 1282 was back on the

13
00:00:49,261 --> 00:00:50,564
ground in Portland.

14
00:00:51,105 --> 00:00:53,244
Eight people had minor injuries.

15
00:00:53,529 --> 00:00:55,394
No one was seriously hurt.

16
00:00:56,049 --> 00:00:58,776
There is no recording of
what was said in the cockpit.

17
00:00:59,239 --> 00:01:03,421
The voice recorder kept running after
landing, and recorded over the flight.

18
00:01:04,061 --> 00:01:07,000
So this investigation
was not built on voices.

19
00:01:07,187 --> 00:01:08,876
It was built on paper.

20
00:01:09,506 --> 00:01:15,052
And the paper leads to a photograph,
taken on a factory floor 108 days before

21
00:01:15,092 --> 00:01:16,193
that afternoon.

22
00:01:16,799 --> 00:01:18,920
It shows exactly what was missing.

23
00:01:19,359 --> 00:01:21,694
We will come to it in about five minutes.

24
00:01:22,366 --> 00:01:25,741
The question was never only
why a door plug failed.

25
00:01:26,066 --> 00:01:29,925
It was why every layer built
to stop it had failed first.

26
00:01:30,551 --> 00:01:32,808
Where did the margin of error run out?

27
00:01:36,916 --> 00:01:42,424
The 737-9 has an opening in each side
of the fuselage, behind the wing, for an

28
00:01:42,464 --> 00:01:44,226
extra emergency exit.

29
00:01:44,868 --> 00:01:49,893
Airlines that do not need that exit
get a plug instead a lighter, simpler

30
00:01:49,933 --> 00:01:51,834
panel that fills the hole.

31
00:01:53,222 --> 00:01:55,602
The plug weighs 63 pounds.

32
00:01:56,132 --> 00:02:00,226
Two kinds of restraint hold it in place,
and the difference between them is

33
00:02:00,266 --> 00:02:01,409
the whole story.

34
00:02:02,096 --> 00:02:06,858
Twelve stop fittings, six down each
side, keep it from moving outward.

35
00:02:07,424 --> 00:02:10,873
Small pins on the plug bear
against pads on the frame.

36
00:02:12,153 --> 00:02:14,782
But stops only work in one direction.

37
00:02:15,328 --> 00:02:19,779
Lift the plug about half an inch,
and the pins slide clear of the pads.

38
00:02:20,209 --> 00:02:22,489
Then the top of the plug can swing out.

39
00:02:23,780 --> 00:02:25,866
That is why there are four bolts.

40
00:02:26,183 --> 00:02:28,498
Two at the top, through the guide tracks.

41
00:02:28,739 --> 00:02:30,939
Two at the bottom, through the hinges.

42
00:02:31,362 --> 00:02:35,312
Each one fitted with washers,
a castle nut and a cotter pin.

43
00:02:36,603 --> 00:02:39,713
Their only job is to
stop the plug moving up.

44
00:02:40,428 --> 00:02:42,081
And here is the margin.

45
00:02:42,808 --> 00:02:47,261
After the accident, Boeing's engineers
worked out how many of those bolts were

46
00:02:47,301 --> 00:02:48,324
really needed.

47
00:02:48,955 --> 00:02:52,607
One lower bolt, on its own,
would have held the plug down.

48
00:02:53,864 --> 00:02:57,899
The two upper bolts, on their own,
would have kept it on its stops.

49
00:02:59,151 --> 00:03:02,439
Four bolts, where a single
lower bolt would have done.

50
00:03:02,967 --> 00:03:05,492
A margin of error, built in steel.

51
00:03:07,128 --> 00:03:09,793
On this airplane, all four were missing.

52
00:03:11,625 --> 00:03:13,975
The investigators could
tell from the holes.

53
00:03:14,329 --> 00:03:16,585
None of them carried a mark from a bolt.

54
00:03:17,020 --> 00:03:21,559
But faint rings around the upper holes
showed that washers had once been there.

55
00:03:21,966 --> 00:03:24,895
The bolts had been fitted
and then taken out.

56
00:03:26,164 --> 00:03:31,243
So the question became: who took them
out, and why were they never put back?

57
00:03:35,715 --> 00:03:41,447
The plug was made by Spirit
AeroSystems, in Malaysia, in March 2023.

58
00:03:42,170 --> 00:03:47,577
Spirit fitted it to the fuselage in
Wichita, Kansas, and on the 28th of July

59
00:03:47,657 --> 00:03:50,581
it was closed with all
four bolts in place.

60
00:03:52,183 --> 00:03:54,851
Spirit's Wichita plant
used to be Boeing's.

61
00:03:55,224 --> 00:04:00,789
In 2005, Boeing sold its operations
in Wichita, and they became Spirit

62
00:04:00,829 --> 00:04:05,693
AeroSystems a major
supplier of 737 fuselages.

63
00:04:06,261 --> 00:04:11,159
By 2023, nearly two thirds of
Spirit's revenue came from Boeing.

64
00:04:12,593 --> 00:04:16,817
The fuselage arrived at Boeing's
factory in Renton, Washington, on the

65
00:04:16,857 --> 00:04:18,398
31st of August.

66
00:04:18,838 --> 00:04:23,483
The next day, Boeing workers found
five damaged rivets in the frame just

67
00:04:23,523 --> 00:04:24,764
forward of the plug.

68
00:04:26,100 --> 00:04:29,370
The rivets had been installed
by one of Spirit's suppliers.

69
00:04:29,862 --> 00:04:33,910
But under Boeing's rules, Spirit's
people were not allowed to take apart a

70
00:04:33,950 --> 00:04:35,353
finished installation.

71
00:04:35,831 --> 00:04:37,768
Only Boeing could open the plug.

72
00:04:38,153 --> 00:04:40,412
And opening it was not routine.

73
00:04:41,010 --> 00:04:46,041
Boeing's own process required a removal
record a document that lists the steps

74
00:04:46,081 --> 00:04:50,691
to restore what was disturbed, and the
quality sign-offs that prove it was done.

75
00:04:52,103 --> 00:04:55,478
For more than two weeks, the
rivets went back and forth.

76
00:04:55,894 --> 00:04:59,487
A Spirit contract mechanic
recorded them as replaced.

77
00:04:59,943 --> 00:05:03,928
The same day, Boeing's quality inspectors
recorded that they had not been

78
00:05:03,968 --> 00:05:06,230
replaced, but just painted over.

79
00:05:06,868 --> 00:05:10,674
Spirit later said the mechanic had
confused this airplane with another.

80
00:05:12,033 --> 00:05:14,920
The request for access
was escalated twice.

81
00:05:15,413 --> 00:05:19,662
On the 17th of September, a note
in Boeing's tracking system read:

82
00:05:20,084 --> 00:05:23,892
if removal needed, a removal
needs to be written first.

83
00:05:25,318 --> 00:05:29,206
The next morning, a Boeing door manager
reported that the door was being

84
00:05:29,246 --> 00:05:30,708
opened by a mechanic.

85
00:05:31,099 --> 00:05:33,666
Which mechanic, the record does not say.

86
00:05:35,218 --> 00:05:38,324
Only Boeing's door team was
allowed to work on a plug.

87
00:05:38,793 --> 00:05:44,122
The man usually given that job had
about twenty-five years on 737 doors.

88
00:05:44,634 --> 00:05:47,689
That day, and the next, he was on leave.

89
00:05:48,241 --> 00:05:51,490
None of the door team on
duty had ever opened a plug.

90
00:05:52,850 --> 00:05:54,286
The plug was opened.

91
00:05:54,559 --> 00:05:56,537
No removal record was written.

92
00:05:57,000 --> 00:05:59,789
No one interviewed said
they knew who opened it.

93
00:06:01,040 --> 00:06:04,925
Later that day, a technician moving
equipment found the plug lying

94
00:06:04,965 --> 00:06:06,307
open on a stand.

95
00:06:06,719 --> 00:06:11,415
He used a strap to pull it up off the
stand, he said, and let it hang with a

96
00:06:11,455 --> 00:06:13,461
gap all round it in the frame.

97
00:06:14,892 --> 00:06:17,579
The next day, the rivet work was finished.

98
00:06:17,961 --> 00:06:22,431
And sometime on the evening shift of
the 19th of September, when no member of

99
00:06:22,471 --> 00:06:25,657
the door team was working,
the plug was closed.

100
00:06:27,083 --> 00:06:31,192
At about twenty to seven that evening,
Boeing workers were texting each other

101
00:06:31,232 --> 00:06:33,877
about putting the cabin
interior back together.

102
00:06:34,612 --> 00:06:36,609
One message carried a photograph.

103
00:06:38,430 --> 00:06:40,726
It shows the plug, closed.

104
00:06:41,030 --> 00:06:43,578
Three of the four bolt
positions are in view.

105
00:06:44,010 --> 00:06:46,778
In each of them you can see nothing.

106
00:06:47,906 --> 00:06:48,906
No bolts.

107
00:06:48,996 --> 00:06:50,012
No washers.

108
00:06:50,132 --> 00:06:51,132
No nuts.

109
00:06:51,183 --> 00:06:52,183
No pins.

110
00:06:54,332 --> 00:06:57,099
No one interviewed said
they knew who closed it.

111
00:06:57,637 --> 00:07:02,704
As a matter of routine, Boeing kept its
factory security video for thirty days.

112
00:07:03,083 --> 00:07:05,048
The accident came long after.

113
00:07:06,485 --> 00:07:10,677
On the 20th of September, a quality
inspector signed off the job.

114
00:07:11,141 --> 00:07:16,049
Her sign-off, she told investigators,
did not involve looking at the airplane.

115
00:07:16,409 --> 00:07:19,917
She checked that every step for
the rivet work had been signed off.

116
00:07:20,351 --> 00:07:24,516
And with no removal record, the
paperwork said nothing about the plug.

117
00:07:26,024 --> 00:07:29,646
By then, the cabin wall was
already in place over it.

118
00:07:30,223 --> 00:07:34,550
Forty-one days later, the airplane
was delivered to Alaska Airlines.

119
00:07:35,820 --> 00:07:39,616
It would be easy to stop there,
with a missing document and a name

120
00:07:39,656 --> 00:07:40,900
no one can give.

121
00:07:41,529 --> 00:07:45,029
The NTSB did not stop there.

122
00:07:46,478 --> 00:07:50,869
The instruction that told workers when
a removal record was needed ran to more

123
00:07:50,909 --> 00:07:52,432
than fifty pages.

124
00:07:52,907 --> 00:07:57,314
The NTSB found it listed more
exceptions than directions.

125
00:07:57,797 --> 00:08:01,242
Boeing had revised it
eleven times in ten years.

126
00:08:01,547 --> 00:08:06,142
It had come up in sixteen regulatory
compliance cases since 2018.

127
00:08:07,683 --> 00:08:12,246
Boeing's corrective actions were
accepted by its regulator, the FAA.

128
00:08:12,927 --> 00:08:18,030
For at least ten years, the investigators
found, those actions were ineffective.

129
00:08:18,570 --> 00:08:24,174
The FAA had five inspectors on the
737 program while this fuselage was

130
00:08:24,214 --> 00:08:25,214
in the factory.

131
00:08:25,851 --> 00:08:30,343
In the twelve months up to the build,
its audits at Renton logged sixty-eight

132
00:08:30,383 --> 00:08:31,666
alleged problems.

133
00:08:31,974 --> 00:08:34,694
Not one of them mentioned
the removal instruction.

134
00:08:36,118 --> 00:08:37,715
Then there were the people.

135
00:08:38,389 --> 00:08:43,878
Before 2018, the 737 line built
about fifty-two airplanes a month.

136
00:08:44,411 --> 00:08:50,064
After two fatal accidents involving
the 737 MAX, Boeing cut production,

137
00:08:50,185 --> 00:08:53,612
stopped hiring, and did not
replace the people who left.

138
00:08:54,085 --> 00:08:58,357
The pandemic took more, particularly
those eligible to retire.

139
00:08:58,844 --> 00:09:02,384
At the low point, the line
built fewer than ten a month.

140
00:09:02,835 --> 00:09:08,615
In 2022, Boeing set out to return
to its old rate, and it hired fast.

141
00:09:09,054 --> 00:09:12,276
Many of the new hires had
never worked in manufacturing.

142
00:09:13,513 --> 00:09:16,686
The head of the 737
program put it this way.

143
00:09:17,171 --> 00:09:22,254
Ten years ago, close to half the workforce
had more than ten years' experience.

144
00:09:22,512 --> 00:09:25,457
Today twenty-five or thirty percent.

145
00:09:26,752 --> 00:09:30,846
Boeing did not assess what that loss
of experience might do to quality.

146
00:09:31,343 --> 00:09:34,728
The NTSB called it a missed opportunity.

147
00:09:36,310 --> 00:09:39,480
So the margin that ran out
at Renton was not steel.

148
00:09:39,898 --> 00:09:43,443
It was a document no one wrote
and the experience that would have

149
00:09:43,483 --> 00:09:44,745
known to write it.

150
00:09:46,120 --> 00:09:50,513
There is one more record a film like
this has to read, and read carefully.

151
00:09:51,094 --> 00:09:56,095
In 2018, Boeing spent $9 billion
buying back its own stock.

152
00:09:56,446 --> 00:10:00,212
That December, its board
approved up to $20 billion more.

153
00:10:00,732 --> 00:10:04,547
In 2019 it bought back $2.7 billion.

154
00:10:05,063 --> 00:10:08,669
By the start of 2020, the
buybacks were suspended.

155
00:10:09,810 --> 00:10:12,438
The investigation report
does not mention them.

156
00:10:12,768 --> 00:10:17,385
And the NTSB said it could not assess
whether adverse pressure existed on the

157
00:10:17,425 --> 00:10:18,588
production line.

158
00:10:19,800 --> 00:10:23,665
So the buybacks belong in this story
as a fact about where Boeing's money

159
00:10:23,705 --> 00:10:25,707
went, not as a cause.

160
00:10:26,033 --> 00:10:29,587
The cause the investigators
found is in the factory record.

161
00:10:33,647 --> 00:10:37,644
Now go back to the plug, closed in
the factory with its bolts gone.

162
00:10:38,173 --> 00:10:41,369
On the ground, a plug
like this is hard to lift.

163
00:10:41,710 --> 00:10:45,698
On its sister plug, on the other side
of the same airplane, with the bolts

164
00:10:45,738 --> 00:10:50,827
taken out on the ground, a 120-pound
upward pull did not move it.

165
00:10:51,134 --> 00:10:52,538
It took two people.

166
00:10:53,815 --> 00:10:56,541
But an airplane in flight
does not sit still.

167
00:10:56,822 --> 00:10:57,948
It vibrates.

168
00:10:57,996 --> 00:10:59,045
It flexes.

169
00:10:59,235 --> 00:11:03,529
And for brief moments, the load on
the plug drops below its own weight.

170
00:11:04,105 --> 00:11:08,609
At the lowest point the analysis found,
the plug's effective weight, the force

171
00:11:08,649 --> 00:11:13,293
holding it down, fell from 63
pounds to about twenty-five.

172
00:11:14,732 --> 00:11:17,440
Each moment like that
could let it rise a little.

173
00:11:17,668 --> 00:11:19,075
Fractions of an inch.

174
00:11:19,417 --> 00:11:23,564
Across 154 flights, it
walked itself upward.

175
00:11:24,689 --> 00:11:29,733
It left a record of the climb: parallel
lines of dust, dirt and petroleum

176
00:11:29,773 --> 00:11:32,635
jelly along the seal, marking its steps.

177
00:11:33,785 --> 00:11:38,075
From the outside, the change at its
lower edge was about half an inch.

178
00:11:38,485 --> 00:11:42,973
Too small, the NTSB found,
to catch on a walk-around.

179
00:11:44,253 --> 00:11:48,338
On the fifth of January, climbing
out of Portland, the plug reached the

180
00:11:48,378 --> 00:11:49,719
top of its stops.

181
00:11:50,097 --> 00:11:52,322
The pins rode up off the pads.

182
00:11:52,615 --> 00:11:55,410
The upper fittings struck
their rollers and split.

183
00:11:55,583 --> 00:11:57,303
The lower hinges twisted.

184
00:11:57,500 --> 00:11:59,324
And the plug left the airplane:

185
00:11:59,585 --> 00:12:01,689
up, out and back.

186
00:12:06,841 --> 00:12:12,584
The NTSB adopted its final
report on the 24th of June, 2025.

187
00:12:13,030 --> 00:12:16,740
Its probable cause names a
manufacturer and a regulator.

188
00:12:17,991 --> 00:12:22,393
Boeing's failure to provide adequate
training, guidance, and oversight

189
00:12:22,473 --> 00:12:26,795
necessary to ensure that manufacturing
personnel could consistently and

190
00:12:26,835 --> 00:12:30,296
correctly comply with its
parts removal process.

191
00:12:31,709 --> 00:12:37,573
Contributing: the FAA's ineffective
compliance enforcement surveillance and

192
00:12:37,613 --> 00:12:39,294
audit planning activities.

193
00:12:40,859 --> 00:12:47,530
The day after the accident,
the FAA grounded 171 737-9s

194
00:12:47,591 --> 00:12:49,594
until their plugs were inspected.

195
00:12:50,087 --> 00:12:54,020
On the 24th of January, it told
Boeing there would be no production

196
00:12:54,060 --> 00:12:55,503
expansion of the MAX.

197
00:12:56,159 --> 00:13:00,515
This won't be back to business as usual
for Boeing, the administrator said.

198
00:13:02,056 --> 00:13:07,492
In June 2024, Boeing's head of quality
told a media briefing that the company

199
00:13:07,532 --> 00:13:09,879
knew the move crew had closed the plug.

200
00:13:10,463 --> 00:13:15,674
The NTSB said its interviews had
identified no one, and cut Boeing off from

201
00:13:15,714 --> 00:13:19,983
its investigative information for
releasing details without approval.

202
00:13:21,350 --> 00:13:25,615
At the end of that month, Boeing
agreed to buy Spirit AeroSystems.

203
00:13:25,901 --> 00:13:30,073
The deal was completed on
the 8th of December, 2025.

204
00:13:30,203 --> 00:13:34,356
Twenty years after Wichita was
sold, it was part of Boeing again.

205
00:13:35,579 --> 00:13:38,376
The fix Boeing and Spirit
designed is simple.

206
00:13:38,701 --> 00:13:43,158
Two extra devices that stop the
plug rising even with no bolts in.

207
00:13:43,535 --> 00:13:47,678
Cabin panels that cannot be fitted
unless those devices are engaged.

208
00:13:47,961 --> 00:13:52,958
And lanyards on the bolts, so a removed
bolt hangs where everyone can see it.

209
00:13:53,381 --> 00:13:58,448
In May 2025, Boeing told investigators
it expected to fit it to new

210
00:13:58,488 --> 00:14:00,491
airplanes in 2026.

211
00:14:01,796 --> 00:14:04,136
But the fix is for one plug.

212
00:14:04,526 --> 00:14:08,962
The question the investigation raised
is about every disturbed installation

213
00:14:09,002 --> 00:14:10,326
on every line.

214
00:14:10,781 --> 00:14:16,210
In March 2024, two months after the
accident, Boeing's own audits on other

215
00:14:16,250 --> 00:14:20,457
production lines were still recording
repeated findings of unauthorized

216
00:14:20,497 --> 00:14:21,659
part removals.

217
00:14:23,081 --> 00:14:27,031
One board member wrote that he was
skeptical Boeing's systems at the time

218
00:14:27,071 --> 00:14:29,176
could have caught errors like this at all.

219
00:14:29,664 --> 00:14:34,391
He also noted that Boeing had already
taken at least twenty corrective actions.

220
00:14:34,769 --> 00:14:39,087
Another member said that, over the
Pacific, it could have been a tragedy.

221
00:14:40,344 --> 00:14:43,559
Four bolts, where a single
lower bolt would have done.

222
00:14:43,879 --> 00:14:45,827
The steel had margin to spare.

223
00:14:46,822 --> 00:14:48,347
The paperwork had none.
